$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X = 1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nGiven that $E[X] = 0.8$ and $E[Y] = 1.6$, find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188846 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y = 2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y = 4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X = 0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X = 2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nGiven that $E[X] = 1$ and $E[Y] = 3.5$, find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188891 :question/problem "A total of $N = 345$ customers of an insurance company took out a particular policy. In a random sample of $n = 120$ of these customers, $87$ declared they were satisfied with the company's service.\n\nFind a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of customers who are satisfied with the service.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0, 1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z_{p}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.282$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.645$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.960$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.326$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2.576$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe required confidence interval is\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}\n$)$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188895 :question/problem "A biology teacher studies the diastolic blood pressure of a population of $N = 450$ male students in a high school. He measured the diastolic blood pressure of a sample of $n = 50$ male students. Given that the population mean diastolic blood pressure, in millimeters of mercury ($mmHg$), is $μ = 70$ with population standard deviation $σ = 8$, what is the probability that the mean diastolic blood pressure of the male students in the sample is more than $68mmHg$?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.05$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.06$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.07$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.08$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-1.9$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0256$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0250$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0244$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0239$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-1.8$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0322$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0314$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0307$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0301$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-1.7$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0401$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0392$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0384$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0375$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188896 :question/problem "An engineer is investigating the weights of a population of $N = 249$ volleyballs produced by a production line in one day. He sampled a batch of $n = 32$ volleyballs. Given that the population mean weight, in grams, is $μ = 260$ with population standard deviation $σ = 8$, what is the probability that the mean weight of the volleyballs in the sample is more than $259g$, but less than $262g$?\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.06$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.07$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.08$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-0.7$ </th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2236$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2206$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2177$ </td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-0.6$ </th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2546$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2514$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2483$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$-0.5$ </th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2877$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2843$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2810$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div></th>\n<th></th>\n<th><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.00$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.01$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.02$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1.5$ </th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9332$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9345$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9357$ </td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1.6$ </th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9452$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9463$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9474$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1.7$ </th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9554$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9564$ </td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9573$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188939 :question/problem "A bookshop owner keeps track of the number of books sold per month starting from the beginning of the year. The table below reports his observations.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-border-top=\"solid\" data-border-left=\"solid\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>January</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>February</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>March</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>April</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>May</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Number of Books</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9, 652$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10, 734$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8, 274$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11, 571$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9, 208$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIn which month did the owner sell the largest number of books?"] [17592186189362 :tutorial/content "This lesson will extend our understanding of place value to larger numbers.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following six-digit number:\n\n$$\n346,209\n$$\n\nThe place value chart for this number is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis place value chart has a **ten-thousands** column and a **hundred-thousands** column, as well as the usual thousands, hundreds, tens, and ones columns.\n\nWe say that $346,209$ has\n\n- $3$ **hundred-thousands**\n- $4$ **ten-thousands**\n- $6$ thousands\n- $2$ hundreds\n- $0$ tens, and\n- $9$ ones."] [17592186189371 :tutorial/content "To compute the expected value of a discrete random variable $X$ given a joint mass function $f(x,y),$ we must first find its marginal mass function $f_X.$ Once the marginal mass function is known, we can then compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X]\n= \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n x_i \\, f_X(x_i).\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nRecall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Since we're only interested in the expected value of $X,$ let's compute the row totals only.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f_X$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.20}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X=2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.80}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the marginal mass function of $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.20$ | $0.80$ |\n|  |  |  |\n\nWe can now compute the expected value of $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.2+1.6 \\\\  & =1.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\textrm{E}[X] = 1.8.$"] [17592186189384 :tutorial/content "We can use unit arguments to make conversions between different units of measurement when more than one conversion factor needs to be considered.\n\nFor example, let's use unit arguments to convert $0.006\\,\\textrm{km}$ into centimeters. We start by recalling the conversion table for length units.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere is no direct conversion between $\\textrm{km}$ and $\\textrm{cm}$. However, we can convert from $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}$, and then from $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{cm}$. Let's write down the conversion factors for both of these unit conversions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}, \\qquad \\dfrac{100\\,\\textrm{cm}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nNow, if we multiply these factors together, we'll get a conversion factor from $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{cm}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m}}{\\text{km}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}{\\text{m}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\text{km}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\cdot  100 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}{\\text{km}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}{\\text{km}}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThis conversion factor tells us that there are $100\\,000\\,\\textrm{cm}$ per kilometer. Notice that the meters unit $(\\textrm m)$ canceled away, leaving only the units we're interested in $(\\textrm{cm}$ and $\\textrm{km})$.\n\nTo convert $0.006\\,\\textrm{km}$ into centimeters, we multiply by the conversion factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.006 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} & \\displaystyle  = 0.006 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}{\\text{km}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.006 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{km}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.006 \\cdot  100 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 600 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{cm}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.006\\,\\textrm{km}$ is equivalent to $600\\,\\textrm{cm}$."] [17592186189386 :tutorial/content "We use place value charts to compare whole numbers with the *same* amount of digits.\n\nFor example, let's compare $54,678$ and $54,378$ using the place value charts below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo compare these two numbers, we compare their place values *from left to right* until we find values that differ:\n\n- The digit $5$ in the *ten-thousands* place is the same for both numbers.\n- The digit $4$ in the *thousands* place is the same for both numbers.\n- For the *hundreds* place, notice that $\\color{blue}6 > {\\color{red}3}$. This means that\n$54,\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}6}78 > 54,\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}3}78$.\n\nIn words, we'd say that \"$54,678$ is *larger than* $54,378$.\""] [17592186189390 :tutorial/content "The advantage of box models over area models is that box models can be used to solve division problems with remainders.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find the quotient and remainder of the following division problem:\n\n$$\n83 \\div 6\n$$\n\nFirst, we write our division in the usual way.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFilling in our box model by applying our usual method, we get the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{5}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The result of the final subtraction is $\\fbox{5}$, not zero.\n- However, we cannot subtract any further since $\\fbox{5}$ is *less than* our divisor $6$.\n- Therefore, $\\fbox{5}$ is the remainder!\n\nThe quotient is the sum of the numbers on top of the boxes:\n\n$$\n10+3=13\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our division problem is\n\n$$\n83 \\div 6 = 13 \\,\\text{R}\\, \\fbox{5}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186189393 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose we want to write the number $237$ in words.\n\nWe start by writing down its place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we write the number in words:\n\n- The number has $\\color{blue}2$ hundreds, so we write \"two\" followed by \"hundred\":$\\qquad$ \"*two hundred*\"\n- The number has $\\color{red}3$ tens and $\\color{red}7$ ones, so we add \"thirty-seven\": $\\qquad$ \"*two hundred thirty-seven*\"\n\nTherefore, the number $237$ written in words is *two hundred thirty-seven*."] [17592186189411 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the following seven-digit number:\n\n$$\n2,402,536\n$$\n\nThe place value chart for this number is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>millions</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundred-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis place value chart has a new **millions** column.\n\nWe say that $2,402,536$ has\n\n- $2$ **millions**\n- $4$ hundred-thousands\n- $0$ ten-thousands\n- $2$ thousands\n- $5$ hundreds\n- $3$ tens, and\n- $6$ ones."] [17592186189412 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to write numbers up to eight digits in words. We start by considering two-digit numbers.\n\nSuppose we want to write the number $56$ in words. We start by writing down its place value chart.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we write the number in words as follows:\n\n- The number has $\\color{blue}5$ tens, so we write \"fifty,\" followed by a hyphen:$\\qquad$ \"*fifty-*\"\n- The number has $\\color{red}6$ ones, so we add \"six\": $\\qquad$ \"*fifty-six*\"\n\nTherefore, the number $56$ written in words is *fifty-six.*\n\n**Watch Out!** Don't forget to include the hyphen!"] [17592186189417 :tutorial/content "The table below gives the spellings of all whole numbers from $1$ to $20$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"4\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Numbers $1$-$20$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">eleven</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">two</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">twelve</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">three</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">thirteen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">four</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">fourteen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">five</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">fifteen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">six</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">sixteen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">seven</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">seventeen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">eight</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">eighteen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">nine</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">nineteen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">ten</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">twenty</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table below gives the spellings of all multiples of $10$ up to $100$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">ten</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">twenty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">thirty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">forty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$50$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">fifty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$60$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">sixty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$70$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">seventy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">eighty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">ninety</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one hundred</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186189434 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to write a number expressed in words using digits.\n\nAs an example, let's write the number \"thirty-nine\" using digits.\n\nThe key is to determine the place value of each part of the number. So, we first break down our number\nas follows:\n\n- \"thirty\" means the number has $\\color{blue}3$ tens\n- \"nine\" means the number has $\\color{red}9$ ones\n\nThen, we write down the place values of \"thirty-nine\" in a place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{9}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"thirty-nine\" written using digits is ${\\color{blue}{3}}{\\color{red}{9}}$."] [17592186189442 :tutorial/content "It's sometimes helpful to switch between different place value representations of the same number.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following three-digit whole number:\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}4}{\\color{blue}7}{\\color{magenta}2}\n$$\n\nThe place value chart for this number is shown below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{purple}4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}7}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{magenta}2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, $472$ represents \"$4$ hundreds, $7$ tens and $2$ ones.\"\n\nHowever, $472$ also represents $472$ ones, because\n\n$$\n472 = \\underbrace{1 + 1 + 1 + \\cdots + 1}_{472\\,\\textrm{times}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \"$4$ hundreds, $7$ tens and $2$ ones\" is the same as $472$ ones.\n\nIn math, we use the word **equivalent** (e-quiv-a-lent) to mean \"the same as.\""] [17592186189444 :tutorial/content "Let's now think about the different place value representations of\n\n${\\color{blue}{1}}{\\color{magenta}{0}} \\, \\textrm{tens.}$\nAs it's written, its place-value chart is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{1}}{\\color{magenta}{0}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are two digits in the \"tens\" column, so we move the first digit $(\\color{blue}{1})$ to the \"hundreds\" column:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{magenta}{0}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"${\\color{blue}{1}}{\\color{magenta}{0}}$ tens\" is equivalent to \"${\\color{blue}{1}}$ hundred.\"\n\nThe equivalence of the two place value charts simply means that a list of $10$ individual blocks of length $10$ (i.e., ${\\color{blue}{1}}{\\color{magenta}{0}}$ tens) can be viewed as a single block with $100$ objects (i.e., ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ hundred):\n\n![](images/b5/b5845c8f94cf6b81e34f8ccfe39c6085327bd7c0e227ac058cb6bae861bce0ca.png)"] [17592186189455 :tutorial/content "We can round whole numbers using a place value chart.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's again round the following number to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n123\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our number's digits in a place value chart. Since we're rounding to the nearest *ten*, we highlight the tens place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (i.e., the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}3$ is *less than* $5$, we round *down*. To do this, we put $\\color{blue}0$ in the ones place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAnd that's it!\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $123$ rounded to the nearest ten is $120$."] [17592186189456 :tutorial/content "We can use a similar method to round whole numbers to the nearest hundred, thousand, ten-thousand, and so on.\n\nFor example, let's round $1,824$ to the nearest hundred.\n\nWe start by writing our number into a place value chart. But this time, since we're rounding to the nearest *hundred*, we highlight the hundreds place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *tens* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}2$ is *less than* $5$, we round *down*. To do this, we put $\\color{blue}0$ in *all* places to the right of the hundreds (both tens and ones):\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $1,824$ rounded to the nearest hundred is $1,800$."] [17592186189458 :tutorial/content "We can use place value to add two-digit whole numbers easily.\n\nAs an example, let's find the following sum:\n\n$$\n24+35\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left) and writing the results below:\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}4} + {\\color{blue}5} = {\\color{blue}9}{:}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}2} + {\\color{red}3} = {\\color{red}5}{:}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{red}5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}9$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n24+35 = 59\n$$"] [17592186189460 :tutorial/content "Let's now calculate the following sum using place value charts:\n\n$$\n37+26\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (ones over ones, and tens over tens), as before:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we proceed by adding the numbers in each place value (from right to left):\n\n**Step 1.** Adding the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}7} + {\\color{blue}6} = {\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1}{\\color{blue}3} = { { {\\color{red}1}} \\textrm{ ten}} + { { {\\color{blue}3}} \\textrm{ ones}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we write $\\color{blue}3$ below the *ones* and **carry** $\\color{red}1$ to the *tens*. Let's write this additional $\\color{red}1$ over the *tens* column.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Adding the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}1} + {\\color{red}3} + {\\color{red}2} = {\\color{red}6}\n$$\n\n(notice that we also add the $\\color{red}1$ carried from the previous step):\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{red}6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n37+26 = 63\n$$\n\nLet's now solve the same problem using the standard algorithm."] [17592186189473 :tutorial/content "We can use place value to subtract two-digit whole numbers easily.\n\nAs an example, let's find the following difference:\n\n$$\n65-42\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (*ones* over *ones* and *tens* over *tens*):\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the numbers in each place value (from right to left) and writing the results below:\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}5} - {\\color{blue}2} = {\\color{blue}3}{:}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 2.** Subtracting the tens, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} - {\\color{red}4} = {\\color{red}2}{:}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n65-42 = 23\n$$"] [17592186189476 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following subtraction problem:\n\n$$\n45-17\n$$\n\nFirst, we line up our numbers (ones over ones and tens over tens), as before:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe have a problem. Since $5$ is smaller than $7$, we cannot calculate $5-7$. So what do we do?\n\nThe answer is to take the first number $({\\color{red}{4}}{\\color{blue}{5}})$, \"borrow\" a ten from the tens place and put it in the ones place, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4tens + 5ones &= 3tens + 15ones\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUpdating the first row in our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we subtract as usual!\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the ones, we get $15-7=8{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Step 1.** Subtracting the tens, we get $3-1=2{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n45-17 = 28\n$$\n\nLet's now solve the same problem using the standard algorithm."] [17592186189481 :tutorial/content "When rounding whole numbers to the nearest ten, we sometimes need to adjust the hundreds, tens, and ones.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's round the following number to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n298\n$$\n\nAs usual, we start by writing our number into a place value chart, highlighting the *tens* place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}8}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}8$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow the usual algorithm:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ in the ones place and increase the *tens* value by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$9\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}+1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- However, $9+1=10$ *tens* is the same as $1$ *hundred* and $0$ *tens*. So, we must write $0$ in the *tens* place and increase the number of *hundreds* by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $298$ rounded to the nearest ten is $300$."] [17592186189491 :tutorial/content "We can also round whole numbers using place value charts.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's again round the following number to the nearest ten:\n\n$$\n127\n$$\n\nWe start by writing our number's digits in a place value chart. Since we're rounding to the nearest *ten*, we highlight the tens place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *ones* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}7}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, since $\\color{red}7$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ into the ones place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Then, we increase the tens value by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $127$ rounded to the nearest ten is $130$."] [17592186189492 :tutorial/content "We can round whole numbers to the nearest hundred, thousand, and so on.\n\nFor example, let's round $261$ to the nearest hundred.\n\nWe start by writing our number into a place value chart. And since we're rounding to the nearest *hundred*, we highlight the hundreds place:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe then look one digit to the right in the chart (this will be the *tens* place) and compare it to the number $5{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}6}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince $\\color{red}6$ is *greater or equal to* $5$, we must round *up*. To do this, we follow two steps:\n\n- We put $\\color{blue}0$ into *all places* to the right of the hundreds (tens and ones):\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Then, we increase the *hundreds* value by one:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"Moccasin\">$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, $261$ rounded to the nearest hundred is $300$."] [17592186189493 :tutorial/content "We can manipulate units the same way as variables, which often provides a convenient way to solve problems. One type of problem where units can help is converting between different units of measurement. \n\nFor example, let's use units to convert $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ into grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we're converting from kilograms to grams, we need a conversion between these two units. According to our table, there are $1000\\,\\textrm g$ per $\\textrm{kg}.$ We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm g}{\\textrm{kg}}\n$$\n\nThis is our **conversion factor.** It's important to write the conversion factor with the units included.\n\nTo convert $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ to grams, we multiply this quantity by our conversion factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.03\\,\\text{kg} & =0.03\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the kilogram units $(\\textrm{kg})$ cancel, leaving only gram units $(\\textrm{g}).$ Consequently, our expression can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.03\\,\\text{kg} & =0.03\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\text{kg}} \\\\  & =0.03\\,\\cancel{\\text{kg}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{kg}}} \\\\  & =0.03\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =30\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.03 \\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $30 \\,\\text{g}.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When multiplying by the conversion factor, the units we want to remove should *always* cancel, and the units we wish to keep should *always* remain. If this isn't the case, we've made a mistake somewhere.\n\n- We can think of the conversion factor as equivalent to the number $1,$ and multiplying a quantity by $1$ doesn't change it. Therefore, multiplying $0.03\\,\\textrm{kg}$ by the conversion factor doesn't change the *size* of the quantity, though it does change the units.\n\nFinally, notice that, in this example, we've converted from a \"larger\" unit of mass $(\\textrm{kg})$ to a smaller one $(\\textrm{g}).$ We'll see how to use units to convert from smaller units of mass to larger units later in this lesson."] [17592186189494 :tutorial/content "We can manipulate units the same way as variables, which often provides a convenient way to solve problems. One type of problem where units can help is converting between different units of measurement.\n\nFor example, let's use units to convert $0.6\\,\\textrm{km}$ into meters. We start by recalling the conversion table for length units.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we're converting from kilometers to meters, we need a conversion between these two units. According to our table, there are $1000\\,\\textrm m$ per $\\textrm{km}$. We can write this ratio as a fraction as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm m}{\\textrm{km}}\n$$\n\nThis is our **conversion factor.** It's important to write the conversion factor with the units included.\n\nNow, multiplying $0.6\\,\\textrm{km}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.6 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} & \\displaystyle  = 0.6 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{km} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m}}{\\text{km}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.6 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{km}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.6 \\cdot  1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 600 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.6\\,\\textrm{km}$ is equivalent to $600\\,\\textrm m$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When multiplying by the conversion factor, the units we want to remove should *always* cancel, and the units we wish to keep should *always* remain. If this isn't the case, we've made a mistake somewhere.\n- We can think of the conversion factor as equivalent to the number $1$, and multiplying a quantity by $1$ doesn't change it. Therefore, multiplying $0.6\\,\\textrm{km}$ by the conversion factor doesn't change the *size* of the quantity, though it does change the units.\n\nFinally, notice that, in this example, we've converted from a \"larger\" unit of length $(\\textrm{km})$ to a smaller one $(\\textrm{m})$. We'll see how to use units to convert from smaller units of length to larger units later in this lesson."] [17592186189495 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use units to convert from larger units of mass to smaller ones. Let's now learn how to convert from smaller units to larger ones.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $600\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to grams. We start by recalling the conversion table for mass units. \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $1000\\,\\textrm {mg}$ per $\\textrm{g},$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{g}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of mass $(\\textrm{mg})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{g}),$ we construct our conversion factor by taking the *reciprocal* of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{1000\\,\\textrm{mg}}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600\\,\\text{mg} & =600\\,\\text{mg}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000\\,\\text{mg}} \\\\  & =600\\,\\cancel{\\text{mg}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000\\,\\cancel{\\text{mg}}} \\\\  & =600\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{1\\,000} \\\\  & =\\frac{600}{1000}\\,\\text{g} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $600 \\,\\textrm{mg}$ is equivalent to $0.6\\,\\text{g}.$\n\nNote the following: \n\n- When we say that grams are a \"larger unit of mass\" than milligrams, this simply means that $1\\,\\textrm g$ is a larger mass than $1\\,\\textrm{mg}.$\n\n- If you forget to take the reciprocal to find the conversion factor, the units will not cancel when you multiply. This indicates that a mistake has occurred. In such cases, it's best to double-check that you're using the correct conversion factor."] [17592186189496 :tutorial/content "The base unit of time is the \"second.\" However, measuring time in seconds is not always the best option in real-world situations. It's often more convenient to write time in \"minutes\" or \"hours.\"\n\nWe can use units to convert between all these different units of time. We can manipulate units the same way as variables, which often provides a convenient way to carry out unit conversions.\n\nFor example, let's convert $3$ hours into minutes. Let's start by creating a conversion table for time units.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Abbreviation</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Second</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{s}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Minute</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{min} = 60\\,\\textrm{s}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hour</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{h}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{h} = 60\\,\\textrm{min}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we're converting from hours to minutes, we need a conversion between these two units. According to our table, there are $60$ minutes per hour. We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{60\\,\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nThis is our **conversion factor.** It's important to write the conversion factor with the units included.\n\nNow, multiplying $3 \\, \\textrm{h}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 3 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{h} & \\displaystyle  = 3 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{h} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min}}{\\text{h}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 3 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{h}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 3 \\cdot  60 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 180 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{min} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3 \\, \\textrm{h}$ is equivalent to $180 \\, \\textrm{min}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When multiplying by the conversion factor, the units we want to remove should *always* cancel, and the units we wish to keep should *always* remain. If this isn't the case, we've made a mistake somewhere.\n- We can think of the conversion factor as equivalent to the number $1$, and multiplying a quantity by $1$ doesn't change it. Therefore, multiplying $3\\,\\textrm{h}$ by the conversion factor doesn't change the *size* of the quantity, though it does change the units.\n\nFinally, notice that, in this example, we've converted from a larger unit of time $(\\textrm{h})$ to a smaller one $(\\textrm{min})$. We'll see how to use units to convert from smaller units of time to larger ones later in this lesson."] [17592186189497 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use units to convert from larger units of length to smaller ones. Let's now learn how to convert from smaller units to larger ones.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to convert $12\\,\\textrm{mm}$ into meters. We start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{m}$, which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of length $(\\textrm{mm})$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{m})$, we need to multiply $12\\,\\textrm{mm}$ by the *reciprocal* of the above:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mm}}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $12\\,\\textrm{mm}$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 12 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mm} & \\displaystyle  = 12 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mm} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{mm}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 12 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{mm}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{\\text{mm}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 12 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{12}{1 \\hspace{0.167em} 000}} \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.012 \\hspace{0.167em} \\text{m} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $12\\,\\textrm{mm}$ is equivalent to $0.012\\,\\textrm{m}$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we say that meters are a \"larger unit of length\" than millimeters, this simply means that $1\\,\\textrm m$ is a longer distance than $1\\,\\textrm{mm}$.\n- If you forget to take the reciprocal to find the conversion factor, the units will not cancel when you multiply. This indicates that a mistake has occurred. In such cases, it's best to double-check that you're using the correct conversion factor."] [17592186192388 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Hours ($x$)</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4.5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5.5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$6$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Grades ($y$)</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$62$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$65$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$77$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$81$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours each of four students spent studying for a biology exam and the grades they obtained on the exam. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{\\,y} = 21.3 + 10x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overline{x}=5,$ and $\\textrm{MSE}=1.375,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\textrm{MSE} \\cdot \\bigg( \\dfrac{1}{n} + \\dfrac{\\overline{x}^2}{S_{xx}} \\bigg) }.\n$$"] [17592186192395 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- Thelma had $225\\,\\textrm{g}$ of jelly beans.\n- The jelly beans were equally divided between $5$ people ($4$ of Thelma's friends, and herself).\n- From her share, she gave $25\\, \\textrm{g}$ to her brother.\n\nLet $B$ represent the number of grams of jelly beans Thelma kept for herself. This is the number we want to find.\n\nNow, we can deduce the following:\n\n- Thelma divided the $225\\, \\textrm{g}$ of jelly beans equally among $5$ people, including herself. Therefore, each of them received $225 \\div 5 = {\\color{blue}45}\\, \\textrm{g}$ of jelly beans.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{blue}5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The weight of jelly beans that Thelma kept for herself, $B$, equals the amount we calculated above $({\\color{blue}45}\\, \\textrm{g})$ minus the amount she gave to her brother $({\\color{red}25}\\, \\textrm{g})$. Therefore, the total number of grams of jelly beans Thelma kept for herself is\n$B = {\\color{blue}45} - {\\color{red}25}$.\n\nCalculating the final difference, we get\n\n$$\nB = 45 - 25 = 20\n$$\n\nTherefore, Thelma kept only $20\\, \\textrm{g}$ of jelly beans for herself."] [17592186192396 :question/worked-solution "Let's start by writing down the important information:\n\n- There are $24$ packs of soda.\n- Each pack contains $6$ soda cans.\n- The fridge has $8$ shelves.\n\nLet $S$ represent the total number of cans the clerk will put on each shelf. This is the number we want to find.\n\nNow, we can deduce the following:\n\n- There are $24$ packs of soda, and each pack contains $6$ cans. Therefore, the total number of soda cans is\n$$\n\\displaystyle 24 \\cdot 6|= 20 \\cdot 6 + 4 \\cdot 6; = 120 + 24; = 144.\n$$\n- The total number of cans $({\\color{blue}144})$ is divided equally among $8$ shelves. Therefore, the total number of cans of soda on each shelf is\n$S = {\\color{blue}144} \\div 8$.\n\nCalculating the final quotient, we get\n\n$$\nS = {\\color{blue}144} \\div 8 = 18{:}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the clerk will put $18$ cans of soda on each shelf."] [17592186192399 :question/worked-solution "Let's write \"${\\color{red}{9}}{\\color{blue}{4}}{\\color{magenta}{7}}$ tens\" in a place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{9}}{\\color{blue}{4}}{\\color{magenta}{7}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are three digits in the \"tens\" column. Therefore,\n\n- we move the first digit $({\\color{red}{9}})$ two places to the left (to the \"thousands\" column), and\n- we move the second digit $({\\color{blue}{4}})$ one place to the left (to the \"hundreds\" column).\n\nThis gives the following place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{9}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{magenta}{7}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"${\\color{red}{9}}{\\color{blue}{4}}{\\color{magenta}{7}}$ tens\" is equivalent to \"${\\color{red}{9}}$ thousands, ${\\color{blue}{4}}$ hundreds, and ${\\color{magenta}{7}}$ tens.\""] [17592186192408 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[-2,2]$.\nEstimate the area under the curve $y = (x+3) \\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[-2,2]$ using a *right* Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186192408 :question/worked-solution "The area under the curve\n\n$$\ny = (x+3) \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nover the interval $[-2,2]$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\int_{-2}^{2} (x+3) \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\ng(x) = (x+3) \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nSince $f(x) > 0$, we have that $g(x) > 0$ for all $x\\in [-2,2]$. Therefore, the area under the curve $y=g(x)$ can be approximated using a Riemann sum.\n\nThe (regular) step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{2 - (-2)}{4} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $\\Delta x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^{2} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x \\approx (g(-1) + g(0) + g(1) + g(2)) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x) = (x+3) \\cdot f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{-2}^{2}(x + 3) \\cdot f(x)dx \\\\\n&\\approx (8 + 9 + 4 + 10) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 31 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 31\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192409 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative $f'(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,4]$.\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=4$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\sqrt{11}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\sqrt{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Hint: The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle L = \\displaystyle \\int_a^b \\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186192409 :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\int_1^4 \\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^2}\n$$\n\nThe (regular) step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{4-1}{3} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $\\Delta x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_1^4 g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x \\approx (g(2) + g(3) + g(4)) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f'(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3\\sqrt{11}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\sqrt{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x) = \\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\sqrt{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int_{1}^{4}g(x)dx \\\\\n&\\approx (g(2) + g(3) + g(4)) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (10 + 1 + 2) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 13 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192420 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,9]$.\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=9$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle V = \\pi \\displaystyle \\int_a^b [f(x)]^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186192420 :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=1$ and $x=9$ is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV = \\pi \\int_1^{9} [f(x)]^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\left(f(x)\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe (regular) step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{9-1}{4} = 2\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $\\Delta x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi \\int_1^{9} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x \\approx \\pi\\cdot (g(1) + g(3) + g(5) + g(7)) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x) = \\left(f(x)\\right)^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= π\\int_{1}^{9}g(x)dx \\\\\n&\\approx π \\cdot (g(1) + g(3) + g(5) + g(7)) \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= π \\cdot (1 + 9 + 1 + 0) \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= π \\cdot 11 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 22π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192429 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=x^4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 4x^3\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\dfrac 14 \\textrm{d}u =x^3\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty$ when $x\\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^4$ is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{2}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{{2}^{-u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{2}^{-u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{2^x},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = \\infty\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{2^{x}} = 0\n$$\n\n![](images/19/1969145d74662468ec23d458ac1ad0194d51cbf6b026718b7232466e9357efdf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{x}^{3}\\cdot {2}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{a}}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192436 :question/worked-solution "Remember that a full turn has a measure of $360^\\circ$.\n\nTo find the number of $8^\\circ$ angles needed to make a complete turn, we need to find the value of $360 \\div 8$.\n\nWe can find the value of $360 \\div 8$ using a box model:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$40$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the box model,\n\n$$\n360 \\div 8 = 40 + 5 = 45\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45$ angles of $8^\\circ$ are required to make a complete turn."] [17592186192456 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u = x^2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =2x \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u = x^2$ is as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\infty$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(u)]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\arctan(x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -\\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{x \\to \\infty} \\left[ \\arctan(x) \\right] = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/46/46e6cf2950c99831a7445a862fb36a6a7b8f9233a82e49c7cf5da4a383ec4756.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186192526 :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 15$ and $n_y = 16$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. You're given that unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are $\\overline{x}=30$ and $\\overline{y}=31$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=1^2$ and $s_y^2=3^2$.\n\nConduct a hypothesis test at the $1\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is smaller than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>t_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.761\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.145\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.624\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.977\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.341\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.753\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.131\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.602\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.947\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$16$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.337\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.746\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.120\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.583\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.921\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186192526 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the populations are normally distributed, but we do not know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x < \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= min(15 - 1, 16 - 1) \\\\\n&= min(14, 15) \\\\\n&= 14\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (30 - 31 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{1^{2}}{15} + \\frac{3^{2}}{16}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 1.261\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, the $1\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 14$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 2.624\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.761\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.145\\,$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\,\\color{blue}2.624\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.977\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.341\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.753\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.131\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.602\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.947\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$16$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.337\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.746\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.120\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.583\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.921\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHowever, since the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x < \\mu_y$, we must consider the left tail. We do this using the symmetry of the $t$-distribution. So, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\leq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2.624}\n$$\n\nOur test statistic $(-1.261)$ does not lie in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85dbb739807649887fa53b60e9df4d281e982ecc5e39ef32eee3653302902581.png)\n\nSo, we don't reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $1\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x < \\mu_y$.*"] [17592186192527 :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 15$ and $n_y = 12$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. You're given that unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are $\\overline{x}=8$ and $\\overline{y}=9$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=0.75^2$ and $s_y^2=1.2^2$.\n\nCarry out a hypothesis test at the $5\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ does not equal the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>t_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.372\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.812\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.228\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.764\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.169\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.363\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.796\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.201\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.718\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.106\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$12$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.356\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.782\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.179\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.681\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.055\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186192527 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the populations are normally distributed, but we do not know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$ is the alternative (two-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= min(n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= min(15 - 1, 12 - 1) \\\\\n&= min(14, 11) \\\\\n&= 11\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (8 - 9 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75^{2}}{15} + \\frac{1.2^{2}}{12}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 2.520\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, the $5\\%$ *two-tailed* critical value (the same as the\n\n$$\n5\\% \\div 2 = 2.5\\%\n$$\n\n*one-tailed* critical value) for $k = 11$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 2.101\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.372\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.812\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.228\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.764\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.169\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.363\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.796\\,$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\,\\color{blue}2.201\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.718\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.106\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$12$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.356\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.782\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.179\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.681\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.055\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we are considering both tails, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\leq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2.201} \\qquad\\textrm{or}\\qquad T \\geq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2.201}\n$$\n\nOur test statistic $(-2.520)$ lies in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66df920face74df61db66b8ab8e4d9ebc454972869bb417028fd29d633222c4e.png)\n\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x \\neq \\mu_y$.*"] [17592186192528 :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 42$ and $n_y = 47$ from independent populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. You're given that unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are $\\overline{x}=2.1$ and $\\overline{y}=3.8$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=8^2$ and $s_y^2=3^2$.\n\nCarry out a hypothesis test at the $5\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is smaller than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>t_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$40$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.303\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.684\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.021\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.423\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.704\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$41$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.303\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.683\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.020\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.421\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.701\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$42$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.302\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.682\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.018\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.418\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.698\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186192528 :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$. However, the sample sizes\n\n$$\nn_x = 42 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn_y = 47 \\geq 30\n$$\n\nare *sufficiently large.* Therefore, we can apply the central limit theorem.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated using a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x < \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= min(n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= min(42 - 1, 47 - 1) \\\\\n&= min(41, 46) \\\\\n&= 41\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (2.1 - 3.8 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{8^{2}}{42} + \\frac{3^{2}}{47}}) \\\\\n&\\approx - 1.298\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, the $5\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 41$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 1.683\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$40$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.303\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.684\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.021\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.423\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.704\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$41$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.303\\,$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\color{blue}\\,1.683\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.020\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.421\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.701\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$42$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.302\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.682\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.018\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.418\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.698\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nHowever, since the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x < \\mu_y$, we must consider the left tail. We do this using the symmetry of the normal distribution. So, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\leq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-1.683}\n$$\n\nOur test statistic $(-1.298)$ lies outside the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd12bb6686f2b79a3e4c96a95dad4179d0dab57957da96fd7177f6fd6d4f7add.png)\n\nSo, we don't reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude the following:\n\n> *There is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{insufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $5\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x < \\mu_y$.*"] [17592186192550 :question/worked-solution "Let's write \"$15$ tens\" in a place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n15 \\, \\textrm{tens} = {\\color{red}{14}} \\, \\textrm{tens} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\, \\textrm{ten}\n$$\n\nwe can borrow $\\color{blue}1$ from tens place and convert it into ones:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{14}}+{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue} 10 \\, \\textrm{ones} \\rightarrow}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis gives the following place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}10}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"$15$ tens\" is equivalent to \"$14$ tens and $\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{10}$ ones.\""] [17592186192551 :question/worked-solution "Let's write \"$5$ tens and $5$ ones\" in a place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n5 \\, \\textrm{tens} = {\\color{red}{4}} \\, \\textrm{tens} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\, \\textrm{ten}\n$$\n\nwe can borrow $\\color{blue}1$ from tens place and convert it into ones:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{4}}+{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue} 10 \\, \\textrm{ones} \\rightarrow}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThis gives the following place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{4}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}15}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"$5$ tens and $5$ ones\" is equivalent to \"$4$ tens and $\\bbox[2pt, white, border: 1pt solid black]{15}$ ones\""] [17592186192597 :question/problem "Consider four random samples from four populations having the same variance. The following table gives the sample sizes $n$ and the sample variances $s^2$ for each sample. Rounded to two decimal places, what is the pooled estimate of the variance?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 3</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$n$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$s^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186192598 :question/problem "Consider two independent random samples from normal populations. The summary statistics of these samples are given in the table below, where $n$ is the size of each sample, $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean, and $s^2$ is the sample variance.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$n$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\overline{x}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$s^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4^2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFind a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.001$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$33$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.692\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.035\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.356\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$34$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.691\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.032\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.348\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$35$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.690\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.030\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.340\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$36$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.688\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.028\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.333\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$37$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.687\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.026\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.326\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2} \\right)^2}{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^4}{n_1^2(n_1-1)} + \\dfrac{s_2^4}{n_2^2(n_2-1)}\\right)}.\n$$"] [17592186192599 :question/problem "The length of matchsticks, in millimeters, produced by machines X and Y are known to be normally distributed with means $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$. Engineers sampled matchsticks from both machines. The summary statistics of these samples are given in the table below, where $n$ is the number of matchsticks in each sample, in millimeters, $\\overline{x}$ is the sample mean length, and $s^2$ is the sample variance.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Machine X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Machine Y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$n$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$152$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$164$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\overline{x}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$53$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$52$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$s^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.5^2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1^2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAssuming that the samples are independent, which statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the mean lengths are approximately $1 \\pm 0.375$.\n2. All values contained within the $99\\%$ confidence interval are positive.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean lengths of matchsticks produced by the machines are not equal.\n\n*Hint: Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as follows:*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^2}{n_1} + \\dfrac{s_2^2}{n_2} \\right)^2}{\\left(\\dfrac{s_1^4}{n_1^2(n_1-1)} + \\dfrac{s_2^4}{n_2^2(n_2-1)}\\right)}\n$$\n\n*You may use the fact that $P(T > 2.595) = 0.005$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $260$ degrees of freedom.*"] [17592186192600 :question/problem "A website has an average visitor rate of $16$ visits per hour. A web designer is asked to rebrand the website, but the designer thinks the requested style will negatively impact the visitor rate. They wish to conduct a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ significance level to test whether the visitor rate has subsequently decreased.\n\nA total of $10$ people visited the website in the first hour after launching the rebranded website. The web designer compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the cumulative distribution table below, where $X\\sim \\textrm{Po}(16)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\quad x \\quad$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X\\leq x)$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0433\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0774\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.1270\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.1931\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.2745\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.3675\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.4667\\,$</td>\n</tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAssuming the visits occur independently, complete this hypothesis test and state your conclusion."] [17592186192601 :question/problem "A technology company produced an electronic component at an average rate of $20$ units per day. The company then decided to make some mechanical improvements to increase production efficiency. After the improvements were made, the company supervisor conducted a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ significance level to test whether the production rate had increased.\n\nThe supervisor noticed that $23$ units were produced on a randomly selected day after the upgrade. He compared this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the cumulative distribution table below, where $X \\sim \\textrm{Po}(20)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\quad x \\quad$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X\\leq x)$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.5591\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.6437\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.7206\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.7875\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.8432\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.8878\\,$</td>\n</tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAssuming that the production of electronic components occurs independently, complete this hypothesis test and state your conclusion."] [17592186192605 :question/problem "Consider two paired samples of size $n=150$ from the random variables $X$ and $Y$. Given that the mean of the differences $d_i = x_i - y_i$ is $\\overline{d} =5.6$, and the variance of those differences is $s^2=2^2$, find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of $D=X-Y$. You may assume that the differences between the $i$th sample elements $X_i - Y_i$ are I.I.D random variables and each has the same distribution as $D$.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k,p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$149$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.655\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.976\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.609\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$150$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.655\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.976\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.609\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$151$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.655\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.976\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.609\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186192622 :question/problem "Mary owns a pastry shop and decides to use a new muffin recipe. With the previous recipe, $55\\%$ of the customers bought at least one muffin. Let $X$ represent the number of customers that buy at least one muffin made with the new recipe in a random sample of $6$ customers. Under the null hypothesis that the proportion of customers that buy a muffin with the new recipe is the same as before, what are the critical values of a two-tailed test at a significance level of $10\\%$?\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the cumulative distribution function of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\quad x \\quad$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X\\leq x)$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0083\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0692\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.2553\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.5585\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.8364\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.9723\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.0000\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186192980 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Age ($x$)</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$7$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$9$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Height ($y$)</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$95$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$106$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$110$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$125$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table above gives the ages, in years, and the corresponding heights, in centimeters, of four children. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\widehat{y} = 80.8 + 4.7x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 6$, and $MSE = 10.1$, find a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 4.303) = 0.025$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n$$\n\\displaystyle SE[\\widehat{β}_{0}] = \\sqrt{MSE \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193021 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0, 5]$.\nEstimate the area under the curve $y = x \\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[0, 5]$ using a left Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193022 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1, 9]$.\nUsing a right Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x = 1$ and $x = 9$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x = a$ and $x = b$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle V = π\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}dx.\n$$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193067 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0, 8]$.\nEstimate the area under the curve $y = x^{2} \\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[0, 8]$ using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{12}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{16}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193068 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0, 15]$.\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x = 0$ and $x = 15$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{4}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y = f(x)$, the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x = a$ and $x = b$ is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle V = π\\displaystyle \\int_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}dx.\n$$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193188 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Number of hours ($x$)</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$2$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$3$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$4$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"><strong>$5$</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Grade ($y$)</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$82$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$90$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$91$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThe table above shows the hours four students spent studying for a math exam and their final grades. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\widehat{y} = 64.9 + 5.6x$.\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $x^(―) = 3.5$, and $MSE = 6.1$, find a $90 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 2.920) = 0.05$, where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is*\n$$\n\\displaystyle SE[\\widehat{β}_{0}] = \\sqrt{MSE \\cdot (\\frac{1}{n} + (x^(―)^{2})/(S_{xx}))}.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193261 :question/problem "Consider three random samples from three populations having the same variance. The following table gives the sample sizes $n$ and the sample variances $s^{2}$ for each sample. Rounded to two decimal places, what is the pooled estimate of the variance?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$n$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$s^{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193264 :question/problem "Consider a random sample of size $n_{1} = 10$ from a normal population and a random sample of size $n_{2} = 5$ from another normal population. Given that the samples are independent, the sample means are $x^(―)_{1} = 6.5$ and $x^(―)_{2} = 8.3$, and the sample variances are $s_{1}^{2} = 0.5^{2}$ and $s_{2}^{2} = 2.2^{2}$, find a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the difference $μ_{1} - μ_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\nYou may need the following percentage points table for the student's $t$-distribution.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k∖p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$2$</td>\n<td>$2.920$</td>\n<td>$4.303$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$3$</td>\n<td>$2.353$</td>\n<td>$3.182$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$4$</td>\n<td>$2.132$</td>\n<td>$2.776$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$5$</td>\n<td>$2.015$</td>\n<td>$2.571$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$6$</td>\n<td>$1.943$</td>\n<td>$2.447$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$7$</td>\n<td>$1.895$</td>\n<td>$2.365$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Hint: Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1))))\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193265 :question/problem "Consider two independent random samples from normal populations. The summary statistics of these samples are given in the table below, where $n$ is the size of each sample, $x^(―)$ is the sample mean, and $s^{2}$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | Sample 1 | Sample 2 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $12$ | $9$ |\n| $x^(―)$ | $15$ | $14$ |\n| $s^{2}$ | $7^{2}$ | $4^{2}$ |\n\nFind a $90 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the difference $μ_{1} - μ_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\nYou may need the following percentage points table for the student's $t$-distribution.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k∖p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$13$</td>\n<td>$1.350$</td>\n<td>$1.771$</td>\n<td>$2.160$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$1.345$</td>\n<td>$1.761$</td>\n<td>$2.145$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$1.341$</td>\n<td>$1.753$</td>\n<td>$2.131$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$16$</td>\n<td>$1.337$</td>\n<td>$1.746$</td>\n<td>$2.120$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$17$</td>\n<td>$1.333$</td>\n<td>$1.740$</td>\n<td>$2.110$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Hint: Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))).\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193266 :question/problem "Consider four random samples from four populations having the same variance. The following table gives the sample sizes $n$ and the sample variances $s^{2}$ for each sample. Rounded to two decimal places, what is the pooled estimate of the variance?\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 1</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 2</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 3</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Sample 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$n$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$s^{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193269 :question/problem "A restaurant owner believes that the mean number of customers per day has changed since he changed the menu. The number of customers per day after and before the menu change are known to be normally distributed with means $μ_{1}$ and $μ_{2}$. The table below shows the summary statistics of several randomly selected days before and after the menu change, where $n$ is the number of days in each sample, $x^(―)$ is the mean number of customers per day, and $s^{2}$ is the sample variance.\n\n|  | After Menu Change | Before Menu Change |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $n$ | $7$ | $7$ |\n| $x^(―)$ | $190$ | $150$ |\n| $s^{2}$ | $20^{2}$ | $30^{2}$ |\n\nAssuming that the samples are independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The end-points of the $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for the difference between the mean number of customers are approximately $40 ± 30.364$.\n2. All values within the $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval are larger than $10$.\n3. The confidence interval data suggests that the mean number of customers per day has increased by at least $10$ since the menu change.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k∖p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.001$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$9$</td>\n<td>$1.833$</td>\n<td>$2.262$</td>\n<td>$4.297$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$10$</td>\n<td>$1.812$</td>\n<td>$2.228$</td>\n<td>$4.144$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$11$</td>\n<td>$1.796$</td>\n<td>$2.201$</td>\n<td>$4.025$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$12$</td>\n<td>$1.782$</td>\n<td>$2.179$</td>\n<td>$3.930$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$13$</td>\n<td>$1.771$</td>\n<td>$2.160$</td>\n<td>$3.852$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Using the usual notation, the number of degrees of freedom can be approximated as the greatest integer less than or equal to*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle ((\\frac{s_{1}^{2}}{n_{1}} + \\frac{s_{2}^{2}}{n_{2}})^{2})/(((s_{1}^{4})/(n_{1}^{2}(n_{1} - 1)) + (s_{2}^{4})/(n_{2}^{2}(n_{2} - 1)))).\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193270 :question/problem "A particular intersection has an average accident rate of $12$ per year. The local authority decided to introduce speed cameras to reduce the accident rate. They conducted a hypothesis test at the $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to test whether the accident rate has subsequently decreased.\n\nA total of $6$ accidents occurred at the intersection one year after the cameras were installed. The local authority compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the cumulative distribution table below, where $X∼Po(12)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0203$</td>\n<td>$0.0458$</td>\n<td>$0.0895$</td>\n<td>$0.1550$</td>\n<td>$0.2424$</td>\n<td>$0.3472$</td>\n<td>$0.4616$</td>\n<td>$0.5760$</td>\n</tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n\nBy assuming that the accidents occur independently, fill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of accidents in a randomly selected $12$-month period, then the test statistic is\n$X \\le 12$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} Since $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $5 \\mathrm{\\% } ,$ there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.Therefore, we {{field-6}} that the accident rate has reduced since the cameras were installed."] [17592186193271 :question/problem "![](images/8b/8bc8663d85551b74a172390cfc9b57441b058a0839d9e2d27937c95e4313e60e.png)\n\nConsider a sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}$ from a random variable $X$, and a sample $y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}, y_{4}$ from a random variable $Y$. Given that the two samples are paired and the differences between the paired observations $D_{i} = X_{i} - Y_{i}$ are I.I.D normally distributed variables with mean $μ$ and variance $σ^{2} = 3^{2}$, find a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for $μ$.\n\nThe sample data is given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{i}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_{i}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025$, where $Z∼N(0, 1)$, as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193276 :question/problem "A coffee shop had an average customer arrival rate of $30$ per hour. The shop owner decided to increase prices in an attempt to make the shop more profitable but he suspects this will decrease the number of customers. He conducted a hypothesis test at the $1 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to test whether the customer arrival rate has subsequently reduced.\n\nA total of $22$ customers arrived at the coffee shop within the randomly chosen $60$-minute period the first day after the prices were increased. The owner compares this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the cumulative distribution table below, where $X∼Po(30)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0039$</td>\n<td>$0.0073$</td>\n<td>$0.0129$</td>\n<td>$0.0219$</td>\n<td>$0.0353$</td>\n<td>$0.0544$</td>\n<td>$0.0806$</td>\n<td>$0.1146$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nBy assuming that the customer arrivals occur independently, fill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of customers arriving in a randomly selected $60$-minute period, then the test statistic is\n$X = 30$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} Since $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $1 \\mathrm{\\% } ,$ there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.Therefore, we {{field-6}} that the customer arrival rate has reduced since the prices were increased."] [17592186193277 :question/problem "After collecting information over the last few months, a plumbing company manager determined that, on average, the company served $6$ clients daily. After performing an advertising campaign, the manager decided to conduct a statistical hypothesis test at the $1 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to see whether there had been an increase in the number of clients served daily.\n\nThe manager randomly selected a particular day and found that $13$ clients were served that day. He compared this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, using the cumulative distribution table below, where $X∼Po(6)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td>\n<td>$0.9574$</td>\n<td>$0.9799$</td>\n<td>$0.9912$</td>\n<td>$0.9964$</td>\n<td>$0.9986$</td>\n<td>$0.9995$</td>\n</tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n\nBy assuming that every client is served independently, fill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of clients served on a randomly selected day, then the test statistic is\n$X = 13$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} Since $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $1 \\mathrm{\\% } ,$ there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.Therefore, we {{field-6}} that the number of clients served per day has increased since the plumbing company performed an advertising campaign."] [17592186193278 :question/problem "Consider a sample $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}, x_{5}$ from a random variable $X$, and a sample $y_{1}, y_{2}, y_{3}, y_{4}, y_{5}$ from a random variable $Y$. Given that the two samples are paired and $D = X - Y$ follows a normal distribution with unknown mean $μ$ and unknown variance $σ^{2}$, find a $95 \\mathrm{\\% }$ confidence interval for $μ$.\n\nThe sample data is given in the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x_{i}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y_{i}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"so