l in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of travel packages sold during a randomly selected week, then the test statistic is\n$X \\ge 5$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} Since $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $1 \\mathrm{\\% } ,$ there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.Therefore, we {{field-6}} that the number of tourist packages sold per week has increased after the travel agency expanded the offer of tourist destinations."] [17592186193295 :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_{x} = 18$ and $n_{y} = 20$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. We wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $μ_{x}$ of $X$ is larger than the population mean $μ_{y}$ of $Y$.\n\nYou're given that unbiased estimates of $μ_{x}$ and $μ_{y}$ from these samples are $x^(―) = 8$ and $y^(―) = 6$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $σ_{x}^{2}$ and $σ_{y}^{2}$ are $s_{x}^{2} = 2^{2}$ and $s_{y}^{2} = 4^{2}$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k∖p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" da