problem "Consider two samples of size $n_{x} = 54$ and $n_{y} = 60$ from independent populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. We wish to carry out the hypothesis test at the $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $μ_{x}$ of $X$ is smaller than the population mean $μ_{y}$ of $Y$.\n\nYou're given that unbiased estimates of $μ_{x}$ and $μ_{y}$ from these samples are $x^(―) = 0$ and $y^(―) = 1$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $σ_{x}^{2}$ and $σ_{y}^{2}$ are $s_{x}^{2} = 4^{2}$ and $s_{y}^{2} = 2^{2}$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k∖p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$52$</td>\n<td>$1.298$</td>\n<td>$1.675$</td>\n<td>$2.007$</td>\n<td>$2.400$</td>\n<td>$2.674$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$53$</td>\n<td>$1.298$</td>\n<td>$1.674$</td>\n<td>$2.006$</td>\n<td>$2.399$</td>\n<td>$2.672$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$54$</td>\n<td>$1.297$</td>\n<td>$1.674$</td>\n<td>$2.005$</td>\n<td>$2.397$</td>\n<td>$2.670$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for the chosen test is $t \\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ **left-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $T \\le$\n{{field-2}}.\n\nAt the $10 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-3}}\nevidence that $μ_{x} < μ_{y}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193299 :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_{x} = 75$ and $n_{y} = 88$ from independent populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. We wish to carry out the hypothesis test at the $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $μ_{x}$ of $X$ does not equal the population mean $μ_{y}$ of $Y$.\n\nYou're given that unbiased estimates of $μ_{x}$ and $μ_{y}$ from these samples are $x^(―) = 32.5$ and $y^(―) = 32$,and unbiased estimates of the population variances $σ_{x}^{2}$ and $σ_{y}^{2}$ are $s_{x}^{2} = 1^{2}$ and $s_{y}^{2} = 3^{2}$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k∖p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$74$</td>\n<td>$1.293$</td>\n<td>$1.666$</td>\n<td>$1.993$</td>\n<td>$2.378$</td>\n<td>$2.644$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$75$</td>\n<td>$1.293$</td>\n<td>$1.665$</td>\n<td>$1.992$</td>\n<td>$2.377$</td>\n<td>$2.643$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$76$</td>\n<td>$1.293$</td>\n<td>$1.665$</td>\n<td>$1.992$</td>\n<td>$2.376$</td>\n<td>$2.642$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for the chosen test is $t \\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately\n\n$T \\le$\n{{field-2}}\nor\n$T \\ge$\n{{field-3}}.\n\nAt the $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-4}}\nevidence that $μ_{x} \\ne μ_{y}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193300 :question/problem "Consider two samples of size $n_{x} = 15$ and $n_{y} = 16$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances. We wish to carry out the hypothesis test at the $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $μ_{x}$ of $X$ does not equal the population mean $μ_{y}$ of $Y$.\n\nYou're given that unbiased estimates of $μ_{x}$ and $μ_{y}$ from these samples are $x^(―) = 9$ and $y^(―) = 12$, and unbiased estimates of the population variances $σ_{x}^{2}$ and $σ_{y}^{2}$ are $s_{x}^{2} = 2^{2}$ and $s_{y}^{2} = 4^{2}$.\n\nThe table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$, where $T$ follows a student's $t$-distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k∖p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$1.345$</td>\n<td>$1.761$</td>\n<td>$2.145$</td>\n<td>$2.624$</td>\n<td>$2.977$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$1.341$</td>\n<td>$1.753$</td>\n<td>$2.131$</td>\n<td>$2.602$</td>\n<td>$2.947$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$16$</td>\n<td>$1.337$</td>\n<td>$1.746$</td>\n<td>$2.120$</td>\n<td>$2.583$</td>\n<td>$2.921$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for the chosen test is $t \\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately\n\n$T \\le$\n{{field-2}}\nor\n$T \\ge$\n{{field-3}}.\n\nAt the $5 \\mathrm{\\% }$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-4}}\nevidence that $μ_{x} \\ne μ_{y}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193405 :question/problem "A coin is flipped $6$ times. Let $X$ represent the number of times the coin lands on heads. Under the null hypothesis that the coin is fair, what is the critical region of a two-tailed test at a significance level of $5 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the probability distribution of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X = x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0156$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0938$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2344$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.3125$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2344$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0938$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0156$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193406 :question/problem "A coin is flipped $8$ times. Let $X$ represent the number of times the coin lands on heads. Under the null hypothesis that the coin is fair, what are the critical values of a two-tailed test at a significance level of $10 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the probability distribution of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X = x)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0039$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0313$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1094$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2188$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2734$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2188$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1094$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0313$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.0039$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193408 :question/problem "In a particular region, the average number of storms in September over the past decade was $5$. A meteorologist wants to test if the number of storms has changed. Let $X$ represent the number of storms in September this year. Under the null hypothesis that the average number of storms hasn't changed, what is the critical region of a two-tailed test at a significance level of $2.5 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the cumulative distribution function of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0067$</td>\n<td>$0.0404$</td>\n<td>$0.1247$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n<td>$0.9682$</td>\n<td>$0.9863$</td>\n<td>$0.9945$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193409 :question/problem "A gym has recently moved location, and the owner thinks this could impact the number of customers. The average number of visitors per hour at the old site was $2.5$. Let $X$ represent the number of customers visiting the gym in the new location during a one-hour period. Under the null hypothesis that the number of customers is the same as before, what are the critical values of a two-tailed test at a significance level of $10 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the cumulative distribution function of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0821$</td>\n<td>$0.2873$</td>\n<td>$0.5438$</td>\n<td>$0.7576$</td>\n<td>$0.8912$</td>\n<td>$0.9580$</td>\n<td>$0.9858$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186193819 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to use the four operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division) to solve real-world multistep problems.\n\nLet's take a look at the following situation:\n\n> *Chloe takes $18$ pieces of candy from a jar and another $15$ from a pack she bought. She plans to divide the candy equally between herself and two friends. How many pieces of candy will each person receive?*\n\nWe start by writing down the important information:\n\n- Chloe took $18$ candy pieces from a jar.\n- She has another $15$ pieces from a pack she bought.\n- She wants to divide all the candy between $3$ people (herself, and two friends).\n\nLet $N$ represent the number of pieces each person will receive. This is the number we want to find.\n\nNow, we can deduce the following:\n\n- Chloe took $18$ candy pieces from a jar, and she has $15$ more pieces from a pack she bought. Therefore, the total number of pieces equals\n$18 + 15 = {\\color{blue}33}$.\n- The total number of candy pieces $({\\color{blue}33})$ is then divided among $3$ people. Therefore, the total number of pieces each person will receive equals\n$N = {\\color{blue}33} \\div 3$.\n\nWe can use a box model to calculate $33\\div 3{:}$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\" data-cell-valign=\"top\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td data-fill=\"#EBF5FB\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\!-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-cell-valign=\"top\"><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! 3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\">$-\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! 3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-left=\"none\">$\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\! \\fbox{0}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-right=\"none\" data-border-bottom=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to our model,\n\n$$\nN = 33\\div 3 = 10+1 = 11\n$$\n\nTherefore, each person will receive $11$ candy pieces."] [17592186193824 :tutorial/content "When there are more than two digits in one place value, it's usually easier to use a place value chart to find an equivalent representation.\n\nFor example, let's find an equivalent place value representation for \"${\\color{red}{5}}{\\color{blue}{0}}{\\color{magenta}{1}}$ hundreds\".\n\nWe start with its place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{5}}{\\color{blue}{0}}{\\color{magenta}{1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nThere are three digits in the \"hundreds\" column. Therefore,\n\n- we move the first digit $({\\color{red}{5}})$ *two* places to the *left* (to the \"ten-thousands\" column), and\n- we move the second digit $({\\color{blue}{0}})$ *one* place to the *left* (to the \"thousands\" column).\n\nThis gives the following place value chart:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ten-thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>thousands</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>hundreds</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}{5}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}{0}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{magenta}{1}}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"${\\color{red}{5}}{\\color{blue}{0}}{\\color{magenta}{1}}$ hundreds\" is equivalent to \"${\\color{red}{5}}$ ten-thousands and ${\\color{magenta}{1}}$ hundred.\""] [17592186193827 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to use Riemann sums to approximate definite integrals of *composite* functions. As we'll see, this has some interesting applications.\n\nFor example, suppose $f(x)$ is a positive, continuous function. Some values for $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [1,5]$ are given by the following table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's use this data to approximate the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} x^2 \\cdot f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo approximate this definite integral, we first define a new function $g(x)$ as\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2\\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nNow, our task is to approximate\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals.\n\nThe (regular) step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{5-1}{4} = 1\n$$\n\nWe can approximate this integral using a left Riemann sum with $4$ equal subintervals and step size $\\Delta x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^{5} g(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x \\approx (g(1) + g(2) + g(3) + g(4)) \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nLet's add a row to our table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$g(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$50$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\int_{1}^{5}g(x)dx \\\\\n&\\approx (3 + 8 + 9 + 16) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 36\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a right Riemann sum."] [17592186193829 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to write fractions in words.\n\nThe names of the unit fractions with denominators from one to twenty are listed below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>fraction</strong>*</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><em>name</em>*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\phantom{\\dfrac00}1\\phantom{\\dfrac00}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-whole</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-half</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-third</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-fourth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-fifth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-sixth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-seventh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-eighth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-ninth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-tenth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>fraction</strong>*</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><em>name</em>*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{11}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-eleventh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{12}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-twelfth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{13}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-thirteenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{14}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-fourteenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{15}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-fifteenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{16}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-sixteenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{17}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-seventeenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{18}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-eighteenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{19}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-nineteenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-twentieth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\dfrac14$ is commonly referred to as *\"one-quarter.\"*\n- Aside from *\"one-whole,\"* *\"one-half,\"* and *\"one-third,\"* the fraction names follow a pattern, e.g., *\"one-fourth, one-fifth, one-sixth, one-seventh,...\"*. Understanding this pattern makes it easier to remember.\n- **Watch Out!** Don't forget the hyphen!"] [17592186193835 :tutorial/content "Unit fractions with denominators from $21$ to $39$ also follow a pattern.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>fraction</strong>*</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><em>name</em>*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{21}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{22}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{23}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-third</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{24}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-fourth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{25}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-fifth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{26}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-sixth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{27}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-seventh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{28}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-eighth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{29}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one twenty-ninth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n<td><div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>fraction</strong>*</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><em>name</em>*</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{31}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{32}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-second</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{33}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-third</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{34}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-fourth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{35}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-fifth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{36}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-sixth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{37}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-seventh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{38}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-eighth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{39}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one thirty-ninth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Watch Out!** We have excluded\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{30}\n$$\n\nfrom our tables. Fractions whose denominators are multiples of $10$ follow a different pattern. We'll discuss these separately.\n\nThe pattern in these tables extends to denominators of $99$ (excluding multiples of ten). For example:\n\n- $\\dfrac{1}{52}$ in words is *\"one fifty-second.\"*\n- $\\dfrac{1}{65}$ in words is *\"one sixty-fifth.\"*\n- $\\dfrac{1}{88}$ in words is *\"one eighty-eighth.\"*\n\nWhen writing fractions in words, it's a good idea to approach this systematically. Let's see an example."] [17592186193837 :tutorial/content "Finally, unit fractions whose denominators are multiples of ten have their own names. We list the most common below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>fraction</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>name</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{10}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-tenth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{20}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-twentieth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{30}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-thirtieth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{40}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-fortieth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{50}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-fiftieth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{60}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-sixtieth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{70}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-seventieth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{80}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-eightieth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{90}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-ninetieth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{100}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-hundredth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">one-thousandth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186193895 :tutorial/content "Consider two samples of size $n_x = 14$ and $n_y = 18$ from independent normal populations $X$ and $Y$ with unequal population variances, where the unbiased estimates of $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ from these samples are\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=47\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\overline{y}=40\n$$\n\nand unbiased estimates of the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$ are $s_x^2=7^2$ and $s_y^2=8^2$.\n\nWe wish to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10\\%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the population mean $\\mu_x$ of $X$ is larger than the population mean $\\mu_y$ of $Y$.\n\nTo solve a problem like this, we proceed as follows.\n\nWe are told that the populations are normally distributed, but we do not know the population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2$.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{S_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{S_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes, and\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variances.\n\nIn our example, the null and alternative hypotheses are the following:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x > \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nWe can estimate the number of degrees of freedom $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= min(n_{x} - 1, n_{y} - 1) \\\\\n&= min(14 - 1, 18 - 1) \\\\\n&= min(13, 17) \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.\n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y = 0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{s_{x}^{2}}{n_{x}} + \\frac{s_{y}^{2}}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (47 - 40 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{7^{2}}{14} + \\frac{8^{2}}{18}}) \\\\\n&\\approx 2.635\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the critical region corresponding to our significance level.\n\nGiven a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{k,p}) = p\n$$\n\nfor some particular values of $k$ and $p$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.050$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.025$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.010$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.005$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$13$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"PapayaWhip\">$\\color{blue}\\,1.350\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.771\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.160\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.650\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,3.012\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$14$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.345\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.761\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.145\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.624\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.977\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$15$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.341\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,1.753\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.131\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.602\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,2.947\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nAccording to the table, the $10\\%$ one-tailed critical value for $k = 13$ degrees of freedom is\n\n$$\nt \\approx 1.350\n$$\n\nSince the alternative hypothesis is $\\mu_x > \\mu_y$, we must consider the right tail. So, our critical region is\n\n$$\nT \\geq \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.350}\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that our test statistic $(2.635)$ lies in the critical region, as shown below.\n\n![](images/81/813c8270ac07466c74e50fbc9377d83cfad9e773057d40183550d5dbd204868a.png)\n\nSo, we reject the null hypothesis $H_0$. As a result, we conclude that there is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{sufficient}}$ evidence that, at the $10\\%$ level of significance, we have $\\mu_x > \\mu_y$."] [17592186193919 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll describe a procedure known as **borrowing.** Borrowing is used often when subtracting large numbers, as we'll soon discover.\n\nLet's consider the place value chart of the number $30$:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince\n\n$$\n3 \\, \\textrm{tens} = {\\color{red}{2}} \\, \\textrm{tens} + {\\color{blue}{1}} \\, \\textrm{tens}\n$$\n\nwe can write our place value chart as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}2}+{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, since $1$ ten is the same as $10$ ones, we can **borrow** ${\\color{blue}1}$ from tens place and convert it into ones:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{red}2}+{\\color{blue}1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue} 10 \\, \\textrm{ones} \\rightarrow}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we have now have $\\color{red}2$ tens and\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{10}}\\,\\textrm{ones} + 0\\,\\textrm{ones} = {\\color{blue}{10}}\\,\\textrm{ones}\n$$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>tens</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>ones</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">${\\color{blue}10}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, \"$3$ tens\" is equivalent to \"$2$ tens and $10$ ones.\""] [17592186193942 :tutorial/content "We must be extra careful when converting a unit of area to a larger unit.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that a square has an area of $7\\,200 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$. Let's use unit arguments to express this area in square centimeters.\n\nWe start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilometer</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{km}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Meter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{m}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Centimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td>Millimeter</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mm}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSince we're converting from $\\textrm{mm}^2$ to $\\textrm{cm}^2$, we start with the conversion between $\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{cm}$. According to our table, there are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$. We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nThe problem with using this conversion factor is that it does not contain area units. However, we can convert this into area units by squaring it and applying the rules of exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\frac{10mm}{cm}\\right)^{2} &= \\frac{10^{2}mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of area $(\\textrm{mm}^2)$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{cm}^2)$, we need to multiply $7\\,200 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ by the *reciprocal* of the above, namely\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{cm}^2}{100\\,\\textrm{mm}^2}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $7\\,200 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{mm}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{mm}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200}{100}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 72 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $7\\,200 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ is equivalent to $72 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2$."] [17592186198008 :question/problem "On average, $7$ patients per week are admitted to a hospital's emergency room due to traffic incidents. The local authorities decided to install several new warning signs to alert drivers of upcoming dangers.\n\nLet $X$ represent the number of patients admitted to the emergency department due to traffic incidents during a particular week after the new signs were installed. Under the null hypothesis that the number of patients is the same as before, what is the critical region of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $10\\%$?\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **cumulative distribution function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\quad x \\quad$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\quad\\cdots\\quad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X\\leq x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0009$</td>\n<td>$0.0073$</td>\n<td>$0.0296$</td>\n<td>$0.0818$</td>\n<td>$0.1730$</td>\n<td>$0.3007$</td>\n<td>$\\quad\\cdots\\quad$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186198009 :question/problem "An engineer knows from experience that the proportion of defective items produced by a particular machine is $1.5\\%$. The machine is replaced with a new model, and the engineer thinks the proportion of defective items has decreased. Let $X$ be the number of defective items in a box of $500$ produced by the new machine. Under the null hypothesis that the proportion of defective items has not changed, what is the critical region of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $5\\%$?\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **probability mass function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\quad x \\quad$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,0\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,4\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\,5\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\quad\\cdots\\quad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X = x)$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0005\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0040\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0151\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0382\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.0723\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\,0.1092\\,$</td>\n<td>$\\quad\\cdots\\quad$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186198010 :question/problem "A gym owner suspects that the average number of customers per hour has decreased since the opening of a competing gym. Before the competing gym opened, $9$ customers per hour visited the gym. Let $X$ represent the number of customers arriving at the gym in a randomly selected one-hour period. Under the null hypothesis that the average number of customers is the same as before, what is the critical value of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $1\\%$?\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **cumulative distribution function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\quad x \\quad$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\quad\\cdots\\quad$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X\\leq x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0001$</td>\n<td>$0.0012$</td>\n<td>$0.0062$</td>\n<td>$0.0212$</td>\n<td>$0.0550$</td>\n<td>$0.1157$</td>\n<td>$0.2068$</td>\n<td>$\\quad\\cdots\\quad$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186198062 :question/worked-solution "A table showing the metric units of time is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Second</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{s}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Millisecond</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{ms}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, from the given options, the only unit of time is $\\textrm{ms}.$"] [17592186198064 :question/worked-solution "A table showing the metric units of mass is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, from the given options, the only unit of mass is $\\textrm{mg}.$"] [17592186198065 :question/worked-solution "A table showing the metric units of mass is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nSo, $1\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is equivalent to $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}.$"] [17592186198066 :question/worked-solution "A table showing the metric units of volume is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Liter</td>\n<td dat