<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1587$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1562$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1539$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186199014 :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0, 1)$, compute $P(-1.5 < Z < 1.52)$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.00$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.01$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.02$</th>\n</tr><tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1.4$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9192$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9207$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9222$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1.5$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9332$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9345$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9357$</td></tr>\n<tr> <th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$1.6$</th><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9452$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9463$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.9474$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186199019 :question/problem "In a lightbulbs factory, the proportion of defective lightbulbs is $0.05$. The machine's maintenance is intensified, and the owner hopes that this will cause the proportion of defective lightbulbs to reduce. Let $X$ be the number of defective lightbulbs produced in a box of $100$ after the new maintenance procedure is applied. Under the null hypothesis that the new procedure doesn't change the proportion of defective lightbulbs, what is the critical region of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $5 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **probability mass function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X = x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0059$</td>\n<td>$0.0312$</td>\n<td>$0.0812$</td>\n<td>$0.1396$</td>\n<td>$0.1781$</td>\n<td>$0.1800$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186199020 :question/problem "Josh plays chess online, winning $67 \\mathrm{\\% }$ of the games. He has just found a new platform for playing chess and suspects the players enrolled are stronger. Let $X$ represent the number of games Josh will win out of $5$ games on the new platform. Under the null hypothesis that Josh's proportion of won games is the same, what is the critical region of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $10 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **cumulative distribution function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X \\le x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0039$</td>\n<td>$0.0436$</td>\n<td>$0.2050$</td>\n<td>$0.5325$</td>\n<td>$0.8650$</td>\n<td>$1.0000$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186199021 :question/problem "The caretaker of a playground suspects that fewer children visit the park in August than in July. During a July day, $12$ children per hour visit the playground. Let $X$ represent the number of children entering the playground at a certain hour on an August day. Under the null hypothesis that the average number of children that visit the playground in August is the same as in July, what is the critical value of a one-tailed test at a significance level of $0.1 \\mathrm{\\% } ?$\n\n*Hint: You may use the table below, representing the **probability mass function** of $X$ under the null hypothesis.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$P(X = x)$</td>\n<td>$0.0000$</td>\n<td>$0.0001$</td>\n<td>$0.0004$</td>\n<td>$0.0018$</td>\n<td>$0.0053$</td>\n<td>$0.0127$</td>\n<td>$⋯$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186199478 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)= P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nwhich is defined on an open set $D\\subseteq \\mathbb R^2.$ Recall that a function $\\psi(x,y)$ is said to be a **stream function** of $\\mathbf F$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nIf a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y),$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ is called a **source-free vector field**. \n\nSource-free vector fields have some interesting properties, summarized below:\n\n- *The divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ at any point of $D$ is zero:* \n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0\n$$\n\n- *The *flux* of $\\mathbf{F}$ along any closed curve $C \\in D$ is zero:*\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0\n$$\n\n- *Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves $C_1, C_2 \\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that*\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n}  \\,\\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nStream functions for source-free vector fields play a similar role to potential functions for conservative vector fields. So it's worth comparing them side-by-side.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"none\" data-table-align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source-Free</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Stream Function $\\psi$\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf i -  \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Relationship between $\\psi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F$ is tangential to $\\psi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Divergence is zero\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Flux is zero along closed curves\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Flux integrals are path independent\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}s$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n</td>\n\n<td></td>\n<td>\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"left\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th colspan=\"2\" data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conservative</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Potential Function $\\phi$\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf i +  \\dfrac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf j$\n</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Relationship between $\\phi$ and $\\mathbf F$\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\mathbf F$ is perpendicular to $\\phi$'s level curves\n</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">(Scalar) curl is zero\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\"> $\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Circulation is zero along closed curves\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}= 0$\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"left\">Line integrals are path independent\n</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf{r}$\n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186199484 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the minimum and maximum values of the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2x^2+4y^2\n$$\n\nsubject to constraint $g(x,y) = x^2+y^2 \\leq 1.$ In other words, we wish to find the global extrema of $f(x,y)$ defined on the disc of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5ac34145b3911b19eb3775a915e8f0462c78502be6d6292e7682265c86eafce7.png)\n\n  \n\nTo find the extrema, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we find the critical points of $f$ inside the domain $x^2+y^2 < 1.$\n\n- Then, we find the extreme values of $f$ subject to the constraint $x^2+y^2=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\n- Finally, we determine the maximum and minimum values by evaluating $f$ at the candidate points.\n\nRecall that the critical points of a function $f(x,y)$ are the solutions to $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf 0^T.$ Now, since the derivative of $f$ is the transpose of its gradient $\\nabla f,$ the critical points are also given by the solutions to $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0.$\n\nSo, we can find the critical points by solving $\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation has the solution $(x,y)=(0,0).$ Note that this point belongs to our domain.\n\nNext, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)= x^2 + y^2  = 1.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4x,8y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=2\\lambda x \\\\ 8y=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(2-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(4-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $\\left(0,\\pm 1 \\right).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =2$ in the first equation, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(4-2)=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $x^2+y^2=1$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{0}^{2} & =1\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical point $\\left(\\pm 1, 0 \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(\\pm 1, 0), \\qquad (0, \\pm1), \\qquad (0,0).\n$$\n\nNote that we add the critical point $(0,0)$ to the list of the candidates above.\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at these points: \n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$(x,y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(\\pm 1,0)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0, \\pm 1)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$(0, 0)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f(x,y)$</th>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum of $0$ at the point $(0, 0)$ and a maximum of $4$ at the points $(0, \\pm 1).$"] [17592186199492 :tutorial/content "**Measuring** means finding a number that describes the size of something. \n\nThe **mass** of an object measures how much \"stuff\" (or **matter**) an object contains. The mass of an object is connected to its weight. We use **mass units** to specify an object's mass. \n\nThe most popular measurement system within the scientific community is the **metric system**. Within the metric system, the most common units of measurement are called **base units**.\n\nThe base unit of mass is the **gram,** which we denote using the symbol \"$\\textrm{g}$.\" The object below has a mass of exactly $1\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n![](images/dd/dd6ce9605969965944c1b9a7a87e4064bc1c64fb7195ee58b27e744a5e6dfd2b.png)\n\nWe can measure the mass of an object using **weighing scales.** To measure an object's mass, we first place it on one side of the weighing scales. Then, we put $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the other side until the scales balance perfectly. \n\n![](images/dd/dd4da91ec2ee27333843532cbf303da3dbe5eac388c8b14b538b8852159b0f76.png)\n\nIn this example, we have an object on the left scale and sixteen $1\\,\\textrm g$ masses on the right scale. Since the scales are perfectly balanced, the object's mass is $16\\,\\textrm g.$\n\nIt's convenient to define new units of measurement from the base unit. The most common variations for mass units are shown below:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest (or \"heaviest\") unit $(\\textrm{kg})$ to the smallest (or \"lightest\") unit $(\\textrm{mg})$:\n\n- A regular bag of sugar has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\n- A single postage stamp has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm g.$\n\n- A single grain of sand has a mass of around $50\\,\\textrm{mg}.$\n\n**Important**: Each metric symbol for mass ends in \"$\\textrm{g}$\", the base unit."] [17592186199496 :tutorial/content "Another way to measure mass is to use **customary units**. The most common customary units of mass are **ounce**, **pound**, and **ton**. \n\nA table showing some of the most common customary mass units is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Ton</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{ton}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{ton} = 2,000\\,\\textrm{lb}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Pound</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{lb}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{lb} = 16\\,\\textrm{oz}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Ounce</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{oz}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's give some examples of each unit, going from the largest unit $(\\textrm{ton})$ to the smallest $(\\textrm{oz})$:\n\n- A small car could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{ton}.$\n\n- A small bag of rice has a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{lb}.$\n\n- A single slice of bread could have a mass of around $1\\,\\textrm{oz}.$\n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries."] [17592186199505 :tutorial/content "Another way to measure volume is to use **customary units.** The most common customary units for volume are **fluid ounce**, **cup**, **pint**, and **gallon**.\n\nA table showing the customary units of volume is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Gallon</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{gal}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{gal} = 8\\,\\textrm{pints}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Pint</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{pt}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{pt} = 2\\,\\textrm{cups}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Cup</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{cup} = 8\\,\\textrm{fl oz}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Fluid ounce</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{fl oz}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNote that \"gallons\" is the largest volume unit, and \"fluid ounces\" is the smallest. \n\nCustomary units are not standard in the scientific community. However, they are commonly used in the United States and other countries."] [17592186199506 :tutorial/content "Other time units in common usage are **minute**, **hour**, **day**, and **week**.\n\nA table showing our units of time is given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Second</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{s}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Minute</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{min}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{min} = 60\\,\\textrm{s}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Hour</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{h}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{h} = 60\\,\\textrm{min}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Day</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{day} = 24\\,\\textrm{h}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Week</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{week} = 7\\,\\textrm{days}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186199514 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the table that relates metric units of mass.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Name</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Symbol</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Conversion</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Kilogram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{kg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td>Gram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{g}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Base Unit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milligram</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm{mg}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg} = 1\\,\\textrm{g}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n**Important**: Symbols in the metric system follow a consistent set of **prefix rules**, which we explain below. If you find these difficult, *do not worry*! For now, you just need to remember the conversions shown in the table above. \n\nThe prefix rules are as follows:\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm k$\" stands for **kilo**, which means $1\\,000$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{kg} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{g}.$\n\n- The prefix \"$\\textrm m$\" stands for **milli**, which means $\\dfrac{1}{1,000}$. Therefore, $1\\,\\textrm{mg}=\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\textrm{g}.$ \n\nNotice that if we add $1\\,\\textrm{mg}$ to itself $1\\,000$ times, we get exactly $1\\,\\textrm{g}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{1\\,\\text{mg}+1\\,\\text{mg}+⋯+1\\,\\text{mg}}^{1\\,000\\text{ times}} &= \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g}+⋯+\\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} \\\\ &= 1\\,000\\times \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} \\\\ &= \\frac{1\\,000\\times 1}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} \\\\ &= \\frac{1\\,000}{1\\,000}\\,\\text{g} \\\\ &= 1\\,\\text{g}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $1\\,000\\,\\textrm{mg}$ equals $1\\,\\textrm{g},$ as stated in the table."] [17592186199518 :tutorial/content "Recall that the statement \"$A$ and $B$\", represented symbolically as $A \\land B,$ is true if $A$ is true *and* $B$ is true. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nWe can represent all possible permutations of the truth values of $A$ and $B$ and the subsequent truth values of $A\\land B$ using a **truth table**:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,A\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,B\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,A \\land B\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm T$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\textrm F$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nEach row of the truth table contains an input for $A,$ an input for $B,$ and the corresponding output for $A \\land B.$\n\n- The first row says that if $A$ is true and $B$ is true, then $A \\land B$ is true.\n\n- The second row says that if $A$ is true and $B$ is false, then $A \\land B$ is false.\n\n- The third row says that if $A$ is false and $B$ is true, then $A \\land B$ is false.\n\n- The fourth row says that if $A$ is false and $B$ is false, then $A \\land B$ is false."]}
